243
00:15:49,210 --> 00:15:52,310
and that's not necessarily
available to everyone

244
00:15:52,310 --> 00:15:54,530
and insurances may not
cover it for everyone

245
00:15:54,530 --> 00:15:57,430
so you need to just
remember that these factors

246
00:15:57,430 --> 00:16:01,410
can cause biological
variations and you can

247
00:16:01,410 --> 00:16:05,370
make a more informed
decision on what the next

248
00:16:05,370 --> 00:16:11,270
step might be so when
you're thinking about

249
00:16:12,010 --> 00:16:17,490
testing to consider
when trying to determine

250
00:16:17,490 --> 00:16:20,730
the causes of chronic
kidney disease. We

251
00:16:20,730 --> 00:16:23,730
think about imaging
lab tests. There are

252
00:16:23,730 --> 00:16:26,230
genetic tests as well
that would be essential.

253
00:16:27,530 --> 00:16:31,030
Biopsy is not
necessarily one of the

254
00:16:31,030 --> 00:16:32,950
first things that
we would go to,

255
00:16:33,530 --> 00:16:37,950
but imaging, we usually
start with ultrasound. If

256
00:16:38,550 --> 00:16:42,990
move forward to
either a CT or an MRI,

257
00:16:43,050 --> 00:16:46,930
and then ultrasound
-guided biopsies are

258
00:16:46,930 --> 00:16:50,170
usually after imaging
has been done.

259
00:16:54,030 --> 00:16:59,150
So Cadeco has, as a
global organization, they

260
00:16:59,150 --> 00:17:02,230
developed and implemented
this evidence-based

261
00:17:02,230 --> 00:17:05,430
clinical practice guideline
to help improve the

262
00:17:05,430 --> 00:17:07,970
care of people with
kidney disease worldwide.

263
00:17:08,590 --> 00:17:11,910
And they have a lot of
tools that are online.

264
00:17:11,910 --> 00:17:16,890
You can look at their heat
maps at kidney.org, or

265
00:17:16,890 --> 00:17:19,050
you can look at their
evaluation and management

266
00:17:19,050 --> 00:17:21,630
guidelines. Both are
linked here on this slide.

267
00:17:22,550 --> 00:17:25,790
But they've developed tools
to assess and classify

268
00:17:25,790 --> 00:17:29,530
patient risk for chronic kidney
disease and kidney disease

269
00:17:29,530 --> 00:17:32,970
progression, as well as
potential complications. And

270
00:17:32,970 --> 00:17:35,690
based on their guidelines,
the recommendation is that

271
00:17:35,690 --> 00:17:40,690
staging should use GFR in
conjunction with an albumin

272
00:17:40,690 --> 00:17:44,230
-to-creatinine ratio to
determine risk and prognosis.

273
00:17:44,950 --> 00:17:47,350
And so this is what
they call their heat

274
00:17:47,350 --> 00:17:50,270
map. And so if you
look at this table,

275
00:17:50,270 --> 00:17:52,790
you'll see these
columns going down.

276
00:17:52,790 --> 00:17:58,010
And A1, A2, A3 represent
the level of albuminuria.

277
00:17:58,460 --> 00:18:01,970
And you'll see these rows
on the left-hand side.

278
00:18:01,970 --> 00:18:06,160
And these are G1 through
G5, and these represent

279
00:18:06,160 --> 00:18:11,390
the changes to GFR. So G1
is normal or high, going

280
00:18:11,390 --> 00:18:13,750
all the way down to
G5, which is considered

281
00:18:13,750 --> 00:18:17,810
kidney failure. And here
right next to the colorful

282
00:18:17,810 --> 00:18:23,890
boxes, you'll see the level
of the GFR in relation

283
00:18:23,890 --> 00:18:26,390
to that stage. And so
they call it a heat map

284
00:18:26,390 --> 00:18:31,770
because as you go from
left to the right and you

285
00:18:31,770 --> 00:18:34,970
go from top down, you'll
see it becomes redder.

286
00:18:35,070 --> 00:18:39,840
And basically this is
worsening risk, worsening

287
00:18:39,840 --> 00:18:43,870
prognosis, and a worse stage.
And the worst stage here

288
00:18:43,870 --> 00:18:51,250
would be stage G5 with
GFR of less than 15 and

289
00:18:51,250 --> 00:18:54,810
severely increased albuminuria,
so greater than 300.

290
00:18:56,410 --> 00:19:00,550
and they also give you
information within each

291
00:19:00,550 --> 00:19:04,170
little box and they
tell you when to start

292
00:19:04,170 --> 00:19:07,770
treating and so if you're
not sure if you should

293
00:19:07,770 --> 00:19:10,630
be treating then what
you would do is just look

294
00:19:11,310 --> 00:19:15,730
plot out your patient
on the heat map and you

295
00:19:15,730 --> 00:19:19,170
would see that they start
treatment very early This

296
00:19:27,740 --> 00:19:32,480
is a summary table also from
Cadeco's guidelines about

297
00:19:32,480 --> 00:19:37,960
the diagnosis and staging
and a summary of treatment

298
00:19:37,960 --> 00:19:42,250
options or treatments
that should be instituted.

299
00:19:42,250 --> 00:19:45,880
And so if you look at
their current level based

300
00:19:45,880 --> 00:19:49,720
on their GFR and their
albuminuria levels, you

301
00:19:49,720 --> 00:19:54,180
have different
recommendations for treatment.

302
00:19:54,180 --> 00:19:59,420
and then also assessment
of risk is listed here.

303
00:20:01,100 --> 00:20:05,560
Now, this is for the
current level, but if you

304
00:20:05,560 --> 00:20:09,010
repeat this testing
later on, you'll see that

305
00:20:09,010 --> 00:20:13,000
there is a column that
is dedicated to when

306
00:20:13,000 --> 00:20:16,040
you notice a change in
the level of the GFR.

307
00:20:16,440 --> 00:20:20,640
And so at that point, it
would be diagnosis, staging,

308
00:20:20,640 --> 00:20:23,980
and then starting
treatment and reevaluation.

309
00:20:23,980 --> 00:20:25,980
of treatment.

310
00:20:27,500 --> 00:20:33,420
So the recommendation is
that you assess albuminuria

311
00:20:33,420 --> 00:20:36,480
in adults or albuminuria
and proteinuria in

312
00:20:36,480 --> 00:20:41,660
children and their GFR at
least annually in anyone who's

313
00:20:41,660 --> 00:20:44,020
been identified as having
chronic kidney disease

314
00:20:44,020 --> 00:20:47,320
or at risk for developing
chronic kidney disease.

315
00:20:47,760 --> 00:20:50,920
If they are at
higher risk for that

316
00:20:50,920 --> 00:20:52,740
chronic kidney
disease progressing,

317
00:20:52,860 --> 00:20:57,640
then you would reassess
those individuals at

318
00:20:57,640 --> 00:21:01,700
higher risk more often,
so maybe every quarter

319
00:21:01,700 --> 00:21:05,340
or every six months,
because that will impact

320
00:21:05,340 --> 00:21:07,720
when you institute
therapeutic decisions.

321
00:21:08,290 --> 00:21:10,000
If they have a change in

322
00:21:10,000 --> 00:21:12,040
their GFR of greater than 20%,

323
00:21:13,080 --> 00:21:16,400
or it exceeds the
expected variability

324
00:21:16,400 --> 00:21:18,780
that warrants investigation.

325
00:21:18,780 --> 00:21:20,640
Are they taking
a medication that

326
00:21:20,640 --> 00:21:22,160
could potentially cause this?

327
00:21:22,160 --> 00:21:23,890
Have they developed a condition

328
00:21:23,890 --> 00:21:28,120
that could be
causing renal injury?

329
00:21:28,620 --> 00:21:31,520
Is there something
else going on? So

330
00:21:31,520 --> 00:21:33,680
you want to evaluate
your patient.

331
00:21:34,140 --> 00:21:38,580
And then for monitoring,
you keep checking in with

332
00:21:38,580 --> 00:21:40,920
your patient or have them
checking in with you.

333
00:21:41,760 --> 00:21:45,340
But a doubling of the
albumin-to-creatinine ratio

334
00:21:45,340 --> 00:21:49,260
on any subsequent testing
also warrants evaluation.

335
00:21:51,400 --> 00:21:56,820
For cardiovascular risk
prediction to guide preventive

336
00:21:56,820 --> 00:21:59,780
therapies in patients with
chronic kidney disease,

337
00:21:59,780 --> 00:22:02,880
you want to use externally
validated models

338
00:22:02,940 --> 00:22:05,400
that have been developed
within the chronic

339
00:22:05,400 --> 00:22:07,720
kidney disease
population or that also

340
00:22:07,720 --> 00:22:11,680
incorporate GFR and the
measurements of albumin

341
00:22:11,680 --> 00:22:14,680
-to-creatinine ratios
into those guidelines.

342
00:22:14,680 --> 00:22:19,100
So you want to look at
any guidelines from the

343
00:22:19,100 --> 00:22:24,240
American Diabetes
Association, for example,

344
00:22:24,240 --> 00:22:27,700
or the American
Heart Association, or

345
00:22:27,700 --> 00:22:29,500
the American College
of Cardiology.

346
00:22:30,140 --> 00:22:34,380
Any one of them will have
guidelines, and they usually

347
00:22:34,380 --> 00:22:37,760
also incorporate anyone
who has kidney disease.

348
00:22:39,460 --> 00:22:43,320
So symptoms related
to each stage.

349
00:22:44,020 --> 00:22:47,360
In general, patients are
generally asymptomatic

350
00:22:47,360 --> 00:22:50,380
until their GFR
becomes less than 30.

351
00:22:50,380 --> 00:22:54,100
So stage 1 to 3 is
typically asymptomatic

352
00:22:54,100 --> 00:22:56,380
unless they have
underlying causes,

353
00:22:56,380 --> 00:22:58,840
So like diabetes,
high blood pressure,

354
00:22:59,000 --> 00:23:01,700
cardiovascular disease,
autoimmune conditions.

355
00:23:01,720 --> 00:23:04,180
And typically these
patients are found

356
00:23:04,180 --> 00:23:07,920
by lab results. So
they get a lab test for

357
00:23:07,920 --> 00:23:10,660
something else and
this is discovered.

358
00:23:11,140 --> 00:23:13,740
When you get to stage
4, you might have some

359
00:23:13,740 --> 00:23:17,000
electrolyte abnormalities
that start to show up or early

360
00:23:17,000 --> 00:23:20,960
uremia. And when you get
to stage 5, they usually

361
00:23:20,960 --> 00:23:24,540
have kidney failure with
signs of uremia. And at

362
00:23:24,540 --> 00:23:27,340
this point, we start to
look at permanent renal

363
00:23:27,340 --> 00:23:31,080
replacement therapies like
dialysis or transfusion.

364
00:23:32,300 --> 00:23:34,680
Transplant, sorry,
not transfusion.

365
00:23:35,240 --> 00:23:36,720
So what are some of the

366
00:23:36,720 --> 00:23:38,680
presenting signs and symptoms?

367
00:23:39,260 --> 00:23:42,800
When they relate directly
to disruption of kidney

368
00:23:42,800 --> 00:23:45,920
function, that would be
things like hypertension,

369
00:23:46,340 --> 00:23:50,040
urine output, flank pain
that might have edema.

370
00:23:50,320 --> 00:23:54,480
And so you'd be looking
for signs of disease,

371
00:23:54,480 --> 00:23:57,300
but you'd also be assessing
their blood pressure.

372
00:23:57,500 --> 00:24:01,220
Look for signs of dry skin
or signs of scratching

373
00:24:01,220 --> 00:24:03,720
at their skin. Or they
might say to you, like, my

374
00:24:03,720 --> 00:24:06,540
skin's itchy all the time
or it's dry all the time.

375
00:24:06,640 --> 00:24:09,480
Their mucous membranes
might also be dry.

376
00:24:09,480 --> 00:24:12,900
They might also have
chronic joint pain,

377
00:24:12,940 --> 00:24:15,440
and they might also
be short of breath.

378
00:24:17,060 --> 00:24:20,240
So what are the
symptoms of uremia? They

379
00:24:20,240 --> 00:24:22,520
might have nausea or
vomiting, diarrhea,

380
00:24:22,960 --> 00:24:24,760
loss of appetite,

381
00:24:25,200 --> 00:24:27,360
or lack of interest in food,

382
00:24:27,360 --> 00:24:30,080
muscle pain, restless legs,

383
00:24:30,340 --> 00:24:33,220
feeling fatigue,

384
00:24:33,340 --> 00:24:35,200
sleepiness. They
might also have

385
00:24:35,200 --> 00:24:37,860
excessive bruising, confusion.

386
00:24:37,860 --> 00:24:39,550
They might have numbness at

387
00:24:39,600 --> 00:24:42,100
the peripheral extremities,

388
00:24:43,620 --> 00:24:47,520
pruritus, erectile
dysfunction, and amenorrhea

389
00:24:47,520 --> 00:24:50,100
in women who are
still menstruating.

390
00:24:51,060 --> 00:24:54,300
On a urinalysis, if you
did a urinalysis in the

391
00:24:54,300 --> 00:24:58,780
office or a urine dipstick
randomly, these patients

392
00:24:58,780 --> 00:25:01,340
would have protein present
at different levels.

393
00:25:01,740 --> 00:25:04,140
And so at that point,
you'd want to get a

394
00:25:04,140 --> 00:25:06,840
spot urine for protein
to creatinine ratio.

395
00:25:07,340 --> 00:25:10,980
You can do this in place
of a 24-hour collection.

396
00:25:11,040 --> 00:25:15,920
They're also acceptable
in replacing that. And

397
00:25:15,920 --> 00:25:19,400
so if they have greater
than 3, then it's

398
00:25:19,400 --> 00:25:22,200
considered nephrotic
disease. And if it's less

399
00:25:22,200 --> 00:25:27,040
than 2, then it's tubular
interstitial disease.

400
00:25:27,860 --> 00:25:33,240
You can also have the
lab report or do a

401
00:25:33,240 --> 00:25:37,900
microscopic analysis of
the urine, and what you'd

402
00:25:37,900 --> 00:25:42,540
find is either cells
or casts in the urine.

403
00:25:43,940 --> 00:25:48,260
So some lab findings
that you might see. As

404
00:25:48,260 --> 00:25:50,880
chronic kidney disease
increases in severity,

405
00:25:51,200 --> 00:25:53,820
electrolyte abnormalities
and hormonal changes

406
00:25:53,820 --> 00:25:57,500
will occur. The most
notable changes include

407
00:25:57,500 --> 00:26:01,320
hypocalcemia,
hyperphosphatemia, hyperkalemia

408
00:26:01,320 --> 00:26:05,340
with metabolic acidosis,
hyperparathyroidism,

409
00:26:05,540 --> 00:26:07,910
a decreased level
of erythropoietin,

410
00:26:08,460 --> 00:26:11,060
and reduced levels
of vitamin D.

411
00:26:12,400 --> 00:26:15,280
The longer this goes,
the more likely you

412
00:26:15,280 --> 00:26:18,760
are also to see anemia
of chronic disease.

413
00:26:21,220 --> 00:26:24,340
so when you see that
when you see that

414
00:26:24,340 --> 00:26:27,860
someone's hemoglobin has
dropped in comparison

415
00:26:27,860 --> 00:26:31,680
to prior levels this
would be part of a workup

416
00:26:31,680 --> 00:26:34,130
you'd want to check
for iron deficiency

417
00:26:35,240 --> 00:26:39,400
in the initial workup an
erythropoietin level may not

418
00:26:39,400 --> 00:26:45,170
be indicated immediately
But you'd want to start this

419
00:26:45,170 --> 00:26:48,720
workup when the hemoglobin
falls below 11 in a

420
00:26:48,720 --> 00:26:52,040
premenopausal female, so someone
who's still menstruating,

421
00:26:52,040 --> 00:26:55,740
or below 12 in an adult male
or postmenopausal female.

422
00:26:55,900 --> 00:26:57,960
What you might see is a

423
00:26:57,960 --> 00:26:59,900
normochromic normocytic anemia.

424
00:27:00,540 --> 00:27:03,720
They'd have a decreased
hemoglobin in hematocrit.

425
00:27:03,720 --> 00:27:07,100
Their TIBC would be low,
their iron low, and their

426
00:27:07,100 --> 00:27:12,180
ferritin could be high. Their
MCV will look normal, and

427
00:27:12,180 --> 00:27:15,360
their reticulate site count
might be normal as well.

428
00:27:15,860 --> 00:27:20,380
So at that point, if it's
a confounding picture

429
00:27:20,380 --> 00:27:23,690
or you're concerned
about erythropoietin

430
00:27:24,000 --> 00:27:27,360
being part of the problem,
then you would order

431
00:27:27,360 --> 00:27:35,320
an erythropoietin level.
So for hyperkalemia,

432
00:27:37,530 --> 00:27:41,580
the potassium excretion is
dependent on distal flow

433
00:27:41,580 --> 00:27:45,520
and aldosterone excretion.
In oliguric patients,

434
00:27:46,030 --> 00:27:50,700
hypoaldosteronism is
present, and they might have

435
00:27:50,700 --> 00:27:55,240
increased tissue breakdown
or have a high potassium

436
00:27:55,240 --> 00:27:58,760
diet. you want to make
sure that they decrease

437
00:27:58,760 --> 00:28:03,140
that potassium intake and
avoid taking non-steroidal

438
00:28:03,140 --> 00:28:06,460
anti-inflammatories like
ibuprofen, for example.

439
00:28:08,240 --> 00:28:11,540
For metabolic acidosis,
the treatment would

440
00:28:11,540 --> 00:28:14,600
be with an alkali,
and typically it would

441
00:28:14,600 --> 00:28:17,060
be very hard for you
to diagnose this on

442
00:28:17,060 --> 00:28:19,900
an outpatient basis
without getting an ABG.

443
00:28:19,900 --> 00:28:23,240
So we tend to find this
when patients are inpatient,

444
00:28:23,240 --> 00:28:26,860
And they order an ABG,
and they can see and

445
00:28:26,860 --> 00:28:29,320
determine that they have
a metabolic acidosis.

446
00:28:29,740 --> 00:28:34,220
But at that point,
they'd be given sodium

447
00:28:34,220 --> 00:28:37,480
bicarb with the goal
to retain it above 23

448
00:28:37,480 --> 00:28:40,940
-mol equivalents. And
if necessary, the

449
00:28:40,940 --> 00:28:46,100
patient may require
having dialysis as well.

450
00:28:48,220 --> 00:28:50,580
For hyperphosphatemia, the

451
00:28:50,580 --> 00:28:52,840
issue is phosphate retention.

452
00:28:53,040 --> 00:28:56,030
which can then lead to
secondary hyperparathyroidism.

453
00:28:57,140 --> 00:29:00,700
So initially the
PTH will correct,

454
00:29:00,940 --> 00:29:04,500
but over time what will
happen is that a prolonged

455
00:29:04,500 --> 00:29:07,530
high PTH will lead to
renal osteodystrophy.

456
00:29:08,080 --> 00:29:11,180
So the goal then would be
restriction of phosphate

457
00:29:11,180 --> 00:29:16,440
in the diet to 800
milligrams per day at most.

458
00:29:16,500 --> 00:29:20,960
It won't decrease the
serum phosphate levels in

459
00:29:20,960 --> 00:29:23,940
general, but it can
improve the secondary

460
00:29:25,210 --> 00:29:28,040
hyperparathyroidism. And
when we see this, typically

461
00:29:28,040 --> 00:29:31,760
phosphate binders are
prescribed to patients as well.

462
00:29:31,760 --> 00:29:34,400
But if you're at this
point, the nephrologists

463
00:29:34,400 --> 00:29:36,860
are generally involved,
and your patient is usually

464
00:29:36,860 --> 00:29:40,380
also on some form of
renal replacement therapy.

465
00:29:41,300 --> 00:29:45,600
So this table describes what

466
00:29:45,600 --> 00:29:48,320
happens as you
lose nephron mass

467
00:29:49,420 --> 00:29:52,850
and what are some of the
mineral abnormalities

468
00:29:53,220 --> 00:29:56,560
that happen. And so I
encourage you to review

469
00:29:56,560 --> 00:30:00,960
this because it's a nice
outline of what happens.

470
00:30:04,520 --> 00:30:07,790
Okay, so touching on
renal osteodystrophy,

471
00:30:08,420 --> 00:30:12,410
it's caused by that secondary
hyperparathyroidism,

472
00:30:13,520 --> 00:30:16,260
and you would distinguish
this from primary

473
00:30:16,830 --> 00:30:20,640
hyperparathyroidism by the
presence of hypocalcemia instead

474
00:30:20,640 --> 00:30:24,340
of hypercalcemia. And the
hypocalcemia is due to

475
00:30:24,340 --> 00:30:28,560
hyperphosphatemia with the
consequent complexing of calcium

476
00:30:28,560 --> 00:30:32,640
in the serum and the second
part is the decreased

477
00:30:32,640 --> 00:30:35,860
conversion of vitamin D by
the kidneys which is needed

478
00:30:35,860 --> 00:30:38,860
to increase those serum
calcium levels. So the

479
00:30:38,860 --> 00:30:43,120
chronic hypocalcemia
leads to the secondary
hyperparathyroidism,

480
00:30:43,120 --> 00:30:47,300
and it can be associated
with tetany seizures,

481
00:30:47,300 --> 00:30:50,630
QT prolongation, which
would be seen on an EKG,

482
00:30:51,280 --> 00:30:56,800
chvostic sign, which is
the initiation of muscle

483
00:30:56,800 --> 00:31:00,260
twitching when the ******
muscles are tapped, or trousseau

484
00:31:00,260 --> 00:31:02,980
sign, which is the
presence of muscle spasms

485
00:31:02,980 --> 00:31:07,280
when the arm is squeezed.
And seizures associated with

486
00:31:07,280 --> 00:31:11,340
hypocalcemia can lead to
injury, including fractures,

487
00:31:11,340 --> 00:31:15,500
while the QT prolongations
in these patients can

488
00:31:15,500 --> 00:31:18,500
lead to life-threatening
arrhythmias, such as torsade

489
00:31:18,500 --> 00:31:23,220
the point, and can
significantly increase the risk

490
00:31:23,220 --> 00:31:26,580
of sudden cardiac death.
And here you can see these

491
00:31:26,580 --> 00:31:29,980
little empty spots, what
they call brown tumors.

492
00:31:32,040 --> 00:31:36,480
And those are due
to the areas where

493
00:31:36,480 --> 00:31:38,920
there's no longer
calcium deposit.

494
00:31:40,240 --> 00:31:43,620
So renal imaging touched
on a little bit earlier

495
00:31:43,620 --> 00:31:47,500
some ways that you can
assess the kidneys.

496
00:31:48,030 --> 00:31:50,370
You can do an x
-ray, a plain film.

497
00:31:50,460 --> 00:31:53,940
These are better for
radio opaque stones.

498
00:31:54,240 --> 00:31:57,880
They don't necessarily show
you the kidneys. They can

499
00:31:57,880 --> 00:32:02,140
show you nephrocalcinosis,
but the kidneys are not

500
00:32:02,140 --> 00:32:05,360
always outlined very well on
imaging. But if you're looking

501
00:32:05,360 --> 00:32:10,580
for a radio opaque stone,
a plain film is okay to

502
00:32:10,580 --> 00:32:14,720
order. You can do a voiding
systourethrogram. This is

503
00:32:14,720 --> 00:32:18,540
good for someone who is
having difficulty voiding, so

504
00:32:18,540 --> 00:32:21,120
someone with like a neurogenic
bladder, for example.

505
00:32:21,380 --> 00:32:23,740
A renal ultrasound
is very good.

506
00:32:24,240 --> 00:32:27,180
You can check renal
size. You can look for

507
00:32:27,360 --> 00:32:30,560
hydronephrosis. You
can look for cysts, in

508
00:32:30,560 --> 00:32:32,560
particular if you're
concerned about polycystic

509
00:32:32,560 --> 00:32:35,640
kidney disease. You can
also look for tumors.

510
00:32:36,580 --> 00:32:40,120
And then there's
nuclear flow imaging.

511
00:32:40,700 --> 00:32:43,720
So this would
evaluate for renal

512
00:32:43,720 --> 00:32:45,740
artery stenosis, for example.

513
00:32:46,760 --> 00:32:49,140
Then we have CAT scans. You can

514
00:32:49,140 --> 00:32:51,000
also evaluate for
the structures.

515
00:32:51,340 --> 00:32:54,700
Sometimes they're helpful
for differentiating findings

516
00:32:54,700 --> 00:32:57,840
from an ultrasound where
it's not clear what it is.

517
00:32:58,860 --> 00:33:03,540
You have to be careful with
the contrast that's used

518
00:33:03,540 --> 00:33:07,180
as you get more advanced
into chronic kidney disease.

519
00:33:07,180 --> 00:33:09,380
The dye may be contraindicated.

520
00:33:09,720 --> 00:33:12,560
And you also want to be
careful in patients with

521
00:33:12,560 --> 00:33:18,140
diabetes or who are on certain
medications. So iodinated

522
00:33:18,140 --> 00:33:23,380
contrast itself can cause
acute kidney injury,

523
00:33:23,630 --> 00:33:26,980
leading to something called
contrast-associated acute

524
00:33:26,980 --> 00:33:30,260
kidney injury. And the risk
for most people is low,

525
00:33:30,260 --> 00:33:33,360
but if you have advanced
kidney disease, diabetes,

526
00:33:33,360 --> 00:33:37,400
or severely dehydrated,
your risk becomes higher.

527
00:33:38,070 --> 00:33:40,240
And there are some
medications you want to

528
00:33:40,240 --> 00:33:43,260
stop if a patient with
chronic kidney disease

529
00:33:43,260 --> 00:33:45,360
needs to get a CAT
scan with contrast.

530
00:33:46,360 --> 00:33:48,920
metformin is one of
them, or glucophage, and

531
00:33:48,920 --> 00:33:51,520
this is due to the risk
of lactic acidosis in

532
00:33:51,520 --> 00:33:54,560
the patients who have
decreased kidney function.

533
00:33:54,560 --> 00:33:57,500
It's typically stopped
the day before the

534
00:33:57,500 --> 00:34:01,160
CAT scan and then
restarted after the test.

535
00:34:03,240 --> 00:34:06,520
NSAIDs, so like
ibuprofen, for example,

536
00:34:06,760 --> 00:34:10,300
ideally stopped two
days before the patient

537
00:34:10,300 --> 00:34:12,790
has to have contrast,
and then some

538
00:34:12,790 --> 00:34:15,580
diuretics like loop or
thiazide-like diuretics

539
00:34:15,580 --> 00:34:18,220
have to be stopped
at least 24 hours.

540
00:34:18,360 --> 00:34:21,920
And then some of the
renin-angiotensin

541
00:34:21,920 --> 00:34:24,560
blockers, so like ACE
inhibitors or ARBs,

542
00:34:24,760 --> 00:34:27,740
there's kind of ongoing
debate about whether you

543
00:34:27,740 --> 00:34:31,300
should stop these or not,
but some clinical guidelines

544
00:34:31,300 --> 00:34:34,680
will recommend that
you reduce the risk of

545
00:34:34,680 --> 00:34:37,760
contrast-induced nephropathy
if you stop these

546
00:34:37,760 --> 00:34:41,760
medications the day before
they have to get contrast.

547
00:34:42,700 --> 00:34:45,120
So, if you do have
an issue with getting

548
00:34:45,120 --> 00:34:47,640
contrast or it's
contraindicated, then

549
00:34:47,640 --> 00:34:50,620
you always have the
option to get an MRI.

550
00:34:51,120 --> 00:34:54,890
And then we also have
angiography, which

551
00:34:54,890 --> 00:34:57,400
is a good way to
assess for renal artery

552
00:34:57,400 --> 00:34:59,680
stenosis, or probably
the best way.

553
00:35:03,220 --> 00:35:07,440
So, primary prevention of
chronic kidney disease.

554
00:35:08,360 --> 00:35:11,900
the primary prevention
aims to prevent

555
00:35:11,900 --> 00:35:14,560
disease or injury
before it ever occurs.

556
00:35:14,680 --> 00:35:18,100
So, and this is done
by preventing exposures

557
00:35:18,100 --> 00:35:20,580
to hazards that cause
disease or injury,

558
00:35:20,580 --> 00:35:24,200
altering unhealthy or unsafe
behaviors that can lead

559
00:35:24,200 --> 00:35:27,400
to disease or injury, and
increasing the resistance

560
00:35:27,400 --> 00:35:31,080
to disease or injury should
exposure occur. So what

561
00:35:31,080 --> 00:35:34,000
are some of those things
that we do for primary

562
00:35:34,000 --> 00:35:37,080
prevention? Well, first we
screen our patients for risk.

563
00:35:37,080 --> 00:35:40,380
And we identify, so,
medical conditions, what

564
00:35:40,380 --> 00:35:43,640
medications do you take?
What's your lifestyle like?

565
00:35:43,900 --> 00:35:45,900
What's your family history?

566
00:35:45,960 --> 00:35:49,320
What's your exposure
and your occupation?

567
00:35:50,080 --> 00:35:52,960
And then we encourage
healthy lifestyles.

568
00:35:52,960 --> 00:35:56,160
We talk about this a
lot in lectures and

569
00:35:56,160 --> 00:36:00,840
in our labs, like TPR.
So a healthy diet,

570
00:36:00,960 --> 00:36:03,020
appropriate levels of exercise,

571
00:36:03,140 --> 00:36:07,040
maintenance of a healthy
weight, and avoidance

572
00:36:07,040 --> 00:36:11,100
of substance abuse or use
or primary prevention.

573
00:36:11,500 --> 00:36:14,100
We want to identify our
patients that are at

574
00:36:14,100 --> 00:36:16,720
higher risk for chronic
kidney disease and

575
00:36:16,720 --> 00:36:19,720
screen using glomerular
filtration rate and albumin

576
00:36:19,720 --> 00:36:22,420
to creatinine ratios
when appropriate. it.

577
00:36:24,260 --> 00:36:27,100
So beyond high blood
pressure and diabetes, which

578
00:36:27,100 --> 00:36:29,860
we know are the two most
common causes of chronic

579
00:36:29,860 --> 00:36:33,360
kidney disease, risk factors
include being overweight

580
00:36:33,360 --> 00:36:37,060
or obese, their age, we
talked about being 65 or

581
00:36:37,060 --> 00:36:40,620
older, and having a family
history of kidney disease.

582
00:36:42,820 --> 00:36:45,520
Hypertension and
diabetes are the top two

583
00:36:45,520 --> 00:36:47,580
risk factors for chronic
kidney disease, but

584
00:36:47,580 --> 00:36:50,280
the strong majority
of people with these

585
00:36:50,280 --> 00:36:52,880
conditions don't always
get the tests that are

586
00:36:52,880 --> 00:36:55,280
needed to diagnose
chronic kidney disease.

587
00:36:55,580 --> 00:36:58,040
So statistics show that 94% of

588
00:36:58,040 --> 00:36:59,500
people with high
blood pressure,

589
00:37:00,070 --> 00:37:05,460
61% of people with diabetes,
and 60% of people with

590
00:37:05,460 --> 00:37:09,380
both high blood pressure and
diabetes do not consistently

591
00:37:09,380 --> 00:37:15,060
get screened for the risk
of chronic kidney disease.

592
00:37:16,540 --> 00:37:19,860
According to the National
Kidney Foundation, up to 90

593
00:37:19,860 --> 00:37:23,160
% of these patients are
unaware that they have chronic

594
00:37:23,160 --> 00:37:26,640
kidney disease or even if
testing was done for it.

595
00:37:26,640 --> 00:37:31,060
And even if it's done, it
doesn't always lead to a

596
00:37:31,060 --> 00:37:34,420
referral to a nephrologist or
to a discussion about risks

597
00:37:34,420 --> 00:37:39,320
and how to reduce their
risk. So the National Kidney

598
00:37:39,380 --> 00:37:43,060
Foundation developed a
program called CKD Intercept,

599
00:37:43,060 --> 00:37:46,620
whose goal is to improve
primary prevention by helping

600
00:37:46,620 --> 00:37:51,000
communities and primary
providers improve screening,

601
00:37:51,140 --> 00:37:53,480
improve education and
management, and reduce

602
00:37:53,480 --> 00:37:56,940
the advancement of chronic
kidney disease. So if

603
00:37:56,940 --> 00:38:02,080
you're interested, you
can go to www.kidney.org

604
00:38:02,080 --> 00:38:04,720
forward slash
professionals forward slash

605
00:38:04,900 --> 00:38:10,640
ckdintercept to find out
more about that program.

606
00:38:11,180 --> 00:38:14,780
So evaluation of these
patients, you want to

607
00:38:14,780 --> 00:38:18,340
be personalized to that
patient specifically.

608
00:38:18,360 --> 00:38:22,420
Run your risk
assessment and make your

609
00:38:22,420 --> 00:38:25,680
management decisions
taking into consideration.

610
00:38:25,990 --> 00:38:29,440
age, sex, gender, and
these risk factors.

611
00:38:29,500 --> 00:38:32,200
At the extremes of age, the

612
00:38:32,200 --> 00:38:33,780
very young and the very old,

613
00:38:34,360 --> 00:38:37,400
goals, treatment modalities,
and decision-making

614
00:38:37,400 --> 00:38:40,720
might differ because they
might have differences

615
00:38:40,720 --> 00:38:43,380
in their prognosis, in
the treatment options

616
00:38:43,380 --> 00:38:46,760
that are available to them,
and the prioritization

617
00:38:47,020 --> 00:38:51,200
of them in terms of renal
replacement therapy.

618
00:38:54,160 --> 00:38:57,800
So also from the Cadeco
guidelines, here are

619
00:38:57,800 --> 00:39:00,820
some things to take into
consideration when you

620
00:39:00,820 --> 00:39:05,440
are assessing your
patients for the risk of

621
00:39:05,440 --> 00:39:09,060
having chronic kidney
disease, but also take

622
00:39:09,060 --> 00:39:12,760
into consideration
management options as well.

623
00:39:14,340 --> 00:39:17,260
So what do you do?
You've identified your

624
00:39:17,260 --> 00:39:19,600
adult who's at risk for
chronic kidney disease.

625
00:39:19,800 --> 00:39:22,200
So the next step
would be to look for

626
00:39:22,200 --> 00:39:24,180
those markers of
kidney damage. Get your

627
00:39:24,180 --> 00:39:26,860
GFR, get your albumin
to creatinine ratio.

628
00:39:26,920 --> 00:39:30,180
And if you see a GFR
that's less than 60

629
00:39:30,180 --> 00:39:32,540
or an albumin to
creatinine ratio that's

630
00:39:32,540 --> 00:39:35,200
greater than 30, and
they have other markers

631
00:39:35,200 --> 00:39:39,400
of renal damage or
high-risk factors,

632
00:39:39,480 --> 00:39:44,920
then you want to repeat and
confirm. And from there,

633
00:39:44,920 --> 00:39:50,810
you are going to make
decisions on staging, as well

634
00:39:50,810 --> 00:39:55,280
as determination of what
the next steps will be.

635
00:39:56,780 --> 00:39:58,680
So management.

636
00:39:58,920 --> 00:40:02,580
The treatment for patients
with chronic kidney

637
00:40:02,580 --> 00:40:05,880
disease tends to
try to incorporate a

638
00:40:05,880 --> 00:40:09,500
comprehensive treatment
strategy to reduce the risks

639
00:40:09,500 --> 00:40:12,620
that can lead to
progression, but also any

640
00:40:12,620 --> 00:40:16,580
associated complications.
So we want to delay

641
00:40:16,580 --> 00:40:20,560
progression of this to
end-stage renal disease.

642
00:40:20,840 --> 00:40:24,460
We want to treat any
associated symptoms. We

643
00:40:24,460 --> 00:40:27,520
want to avoid damage
to other organ systems.

644
00:40:27,520 --> 00:40:30,240
And we want to listen
to the disability and

645
00:40:30,240 --> 00:40:32,960
effect on the quality
of life of the patient.

646
00:40:33,180 --> 00:40:37,840
And so this little
infographic from

647
00:40:37,840 --> 00:40:40,560
Cadego demonstrates
all of that.

648
00:40:41,520 --> 00:40:44,740
So first things first,
we want to encourage

649
00:40:44,740 --> 00:40:47,340
our patients with
chronic kidney disease to

650
00:40:47,640 --> 00:40:50,340
undertake physical
activity that's compatible

651
00:40:50,340 --> 00:40:53,020
with their cardiovascular
health, their

652
00:40:53,020 --> 00:40:56,120
tolerance to exercise,
and the level of frailty.

653
00:40:56,250 --> 00:40:58,280
So we're not going
to tell our 80-year

654
00:40:58,280 --> 00:41:00,660
-old to go out and
run marathons, but we

655
00:41:00,660 --> 00:41:02,460
might tell them to
go for good walks.

656
00:41:02,560 --> 00:41:03,820
or go swimming.

657
00:41:05,460 --> 00:41:07,960
Cadigo recommends that
people with chronic kidney

658
00:41:07,960 --> 00:41:11,000
disease be advised to
undertake a moderately intense

659
00:41:11,000 --> 00:41:15,060
physical activity for a
cumulative duration of at least

660
00:41:15,060 --> 00:41:19,460
150 minutes per week. So if
you take that 150 minutes

661
00:41:19,460 --> 00:41:22,260
and you divide it out
amongst the days, you'll see

662
00:41:22,260 --> 00:41:25,940
that it comes out to 30
minutes five days a week or

663
00:41:25,940 --> 00:41:28,760
less than 30 minutes if you
divide it over seven days.

664
00:41:29,240 --> 00:41:33,440
They also recommend that
we take into consideration

665
00:41:33,440 --> 00:41:37,620
any other comorbidities or
even access to resources.

666
00:41:37,920 --> 00:41:40,400
They should really
be encouraged

667
00:41:40,400 --> 00:41:42,600
to avoid sedentary behavior.

668
00:41:42,600 --> 00:41:47,660
But if they are at higher
risk of falls, then we as

669
00:41:47,660 --> 00:41:50,780
providers should provide
advice on the intensity of

670
00:41:50,780 --> 00:41:54,680
their physical activity as
well as the type of exercise.

671
00:41:54,940 --> 00:41:59,100
We also want to tell them
to aim to lose weight to

672
00:41:59,100 --> 00:42:04,300
try to get it to an optimal
weight or an optimal BMI.

673
00:42:04,960 --> 00:42:07,680
And we also want
to encourage them

674
00:42:07,680 --> 00:42:10,220
to stop using tobacco products.

675
00:42:10,820 --> 00:42:14,340
You can refer to providers
and to programs. So

676
00:42:14,340 --> 00:42:18,140
there's therapists out
there who help with this.

677
00:42:18,140 --> 00:42:21,560
There's renal or dieticians
who specialize in

678
00:42:21,560 --> 00:42:26,960
renal disease. or other
accredited nutrition

679
00:42:26,960 --> 00:42:32,760
programs often affiliated
with transplant organizations

680
00:42:32,760 --> 00:42:37,200
or programs. Your
pharmacist can also help.

681
00:42:37,440 --> 00:42:41,320
Physical and occupational
therapy can help. Smoking

682
00:42:41,320 --> 00:42:45,980
cessation programs can
help. So developing a list

683
00:42:45,980 --> 00:42:50,120
of these programs is essential
so that you can provide

684
00:42:50,120 --> 00:42:53,580
them to your patient or
refer them appropriately.

685
00:42:55,920 --> 00:43:00,740
So this is the infographic
provided by Kadego, and

686
00:43:00,740 --> 00:43:04,080
you'll see the lifestyle
recommendations for

687
00:43:04,080 --> 00:43:07,930
everyone across the top. And
then you'll also see some

688
00:43:07,930 --> 00:43:11,180
first-line drug therapy
for most patients as

689
00:43:11,180 --> 00:43:14,940
well as some other targeted
therapies listed below.

690
00:43:16,660 --> 00:43:20,620
so the adaptation of a
healthy and diverse diet

691
00:43:20,620 --> 00:43:23,340
with a higher consumption
of plant-based foods

692
00:43:23,340 --> 00:43:26,620
compared to animal-based
foods as well as a lower

693
00:43:26,620 --> 00:43:29,960
consumption of ultra
-processed foods has the

694
00:43:29,960 --> 00:43:33,920
potential to reduce complications
related to progressive

695
00:43:33,920 --> 00:43:36,920
chronic kidney disease
such as acidosis,

696
00:43:36,920 --> 00:43:41,860
hyperkalemia, hyperphosphatemia,
while also having

697
00:43:41,860 --> 00:43:46,040
less risk of a protein
energy or loss or wasting.

698
00:43:47,080 --> 00:43:52,560
For adult patients
who have chronic

699
00:43:52,560 --> 00:43:54,980
kidney disease
stage 3 to stage 5,

700
00:43:54,980 --> 00:43:57,640
the recommendation is
the maintenance of a

701
00:43:57,640 --> 00:44:00,940
protein intake equivalent
to 0.8 grams per

702
00:44:00,940 --> 00:44:04,740
kilogram of body weight
per day in the adult.

703
00:44:04,860 --> 00:44:09,320
You want to avoid high
protein intakes, so greater

704
00:44:09,320 --> 00:44:12,400
than 1.3 grams per
kilogram of body weight.

705
00:44:12,620 --> 00:44:16,440
And for those who are at
risk of kidney failure,

706
00:44:16,440 --> 00:44:19,380
you can tell your
patient about the

707
00:44:19,380 --> 00:44:21,400
importance of lowering
their protein intake

708
00:44:21,400 --> 00:44:23,420
if they're taking
too much protein in.

709
00:44:23,870 --> 00:44:26,460
You do not want to
tell your patient to go

710
00:44:26,460 --> 00:44:29,160
to a very low or ultra
-low protein diet.

711
00:44:32,540 --> 00:44:36,060
In children, we typically
don't restrict protein

712
00:44:36,060 --> 00:44:39,780
intake because it could
impact their growth, and so

713
00:44:39,780 --> 00:44:43,340
we will monitor that with
the nutritionist's help.

714
00:44:43,620 --> 00:44:47,100
In addition to other
recommendations for

715
00:44:47,100 --> 00:44:49,520
protein and food
intake is sodium intake

716
00:44:49,520 --> 00:44:52,320
should be less than
2 grams of sodium per

717
00:44:52,320 --> 00:44:56,240
day in adults with
chronic kidney disease.

718
00:44:56,620 --> 00:45:00,360
And the goal is a
targeted systolic

719
00:45:00,360 --> 00:45:02,810
blood pressure of
less than 120 as

720
00:45:04,000 --> 00:45:06,780
long as it's tolerated
by the patient.

721
00:45:07,060 --> 00:45:13,420
And they also recommend
looking at considering

722
00:45:13,420 --> 00:45:16,040
less intensive blood
pressure lowering therapy

723
00:45:16,040 --> 00:45:19,060
in adults who are frail,
who are at high risk

724
00:45:19,060 --> 00:45:21,920
of falls or fractures,
who have a very limited

725
00:45:21,920 --> 00:45:25,360
life expectancy, or who
could become symptomatic

726
00:45:25,360 --> 00:45:27,790
so they could get like
postural hypotension.

727
00:45:28,840 --> 00:45:32,300
ARBs, ACE inhibitors,
SLG2 inhibitors,

728
00:45:32,560 --> 00:45:35,800
mineral corticoid
receptor antagonists can

729
00:45:35,800 --> 00:45:39,160
help reduce the
